---
import FieldGuideLayout from '../../../components/FieldGuideLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<FieldGuideLayout
  title="Nintendo Nitro graphics formats"
  description="The five Nintendo-supplied formats that hold all the actual 2D art in the game. Standard across DS titles, but Tongari uses them in some specific ways."
  crumbs={[
    { label: 'Field Guide', href: `${base}/field-guide/` },
    { label: 'File Formats', href: `${base}/field-guide/file-formats/` },
    { label: 'Nitro graphics' },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    Nintendo shipped an SDK for DS developers that included a small family
    of graphics formats. Most DS games use them and this one is no
    exception. You'll never see them as standalone files in Tongari —
    they're always packaged inside a RESO container — but they're at the
    bottom of every 2D asset.
  </p>

  <p>
    There are five formats that matter for 2D, with three-letter
    abbreviations Nintendo gave them. Read on for what each one is.
  </p>

  <h2>NCGR — tile sheet graphics</h2>
  <p>
    Pixels, organized into 8×8 tiles. Each pixel is a small integer (an
    index into a palette), not a color. The header tells you how big the
    sheet is, what bit depth it uses (4-bit = 16 colors, 8-bit = 256
    colors), and whether the tiles are stored in the normal 8×8 layout
    or in a linear "bitmap" layout.
  </p>

  <pre><code>{`+0x00  "RGCN" magic (NCGR backwards)
+0x10  "RAHC" magic (the "character" section)
+0x18  u16  tile rows (or 0xFFFF if not declared)
+0x1A  u16  tile cols (or 0xFFFF if not declared)
+0x1C  u32  bit depth (3 = 4bpp, 4 = 8bpp)
+0x24  u32  linear flag (0 = tiled, 1 = bitmap)
+0x30  raw pixels`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    Often the width and height fields are <code>0xFFFF</code> ("not
    declared"). For sprites, the cell size comes from the paired NCER.
    For backgrounds, the canvas is 256×192 from the paired NSCR. You
    have to look at the partner files to know how to interpret the tile
    sheet.
  </p>

  <h2>NCLR — palette</h2>
  <p>
    A flat list of 16-bit BGR colors. 16 colors for a 4-bit palette, 256
    colors for an 8-bit palette. Some palettes hold multiple banks of 16
    colors each, with a separate "palette compression map" telling you
    which bank goes to which slot.
  </p>

  <pre><code>{`+0x00  "RLCN" magic
+0x10  "TTLP" magic (the "palette" section)
+0x18  u32   bit depth
+0x20  u32   palette data size
+0x28  16-bit BGR colors begin here`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    The format is BGR-555 with a leading bit ignored, packed little-endian.
    Index 0 is treated as transparent on the DS hardware for OBJ sprites
    and for any BG that uses priority-0 transparency. That means you
    can't put a "visible" color in slot 0 — if you do, it'll show as
    transparent on the actual hardware.
  </p>

  <h2>NCER — sprite cells</h2>
  <p>
    A definition for how to assemble a sprite from many small "OBJ" chips.
    The DS hardware can place individual chips (8×8, 16×16, 32×32, 64×64,
    or various wide/tall sizes) at specific positions. An NCER cell is a
    list of OBJ entries describing one sprite assembly.
  </p>

  <p>
    Each OBJ entry has its own X/Y offset, size, palette bank, and tile
    index into the paired NCGR. To render a cell, you walk the list,
    look up each OBJ's tiles, and stamp them onto the canvas at the
    given offsets. NCER cells can flip OBJs horizontally and vertically,
    so the same tile can appear multiple times in different orientations.
  </p>

  <h2>NSCR — background tilemap</h2>
  <p>
    A 32×24 grid of 16-bit cells covering a full 256×192 screen. Each
    cell points at a tile in the NCGR, with bits for palette bank and
    horizontal/vertical flip.
  </p>

  <p>
    The cell bits, low to high:
  </p>

  <pre><code>{`bits 0-9    tile index (0..1023)
bit 10      horizontal flip
bit 11      vertical flip
bits 12-15  palette bank (0..15)`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    To render a background, you walk the grid row by row, look up each
    cell's tile from the NCGR, apply the flips, and draw it with the
    chosen palette bank's 16 colors.
  </p>

  <h2>NANR — sprite animations</h2>
  <p>
    A list of frames over time, each frame being one or more NCER cells.
    The format defines timing and frame sequences. We can read NANRs but
    don't currently play them back — each cell renders as a static frame
    in our extraction pipeline.
  </p>

  <h2>NCBR — the linear NCGR variant</h2>
  <p>
    A flag on the NCGR header that says "this sheet isn't tiled — it's
    a flat bitmap." Used occasionally. Most things are tiled.
  </p>

  <h2>The other Nitro formats</h2>
  <p>
    NFTR (fonts) and the 3D formats (NSBMD models, NSBTX textures,
    NSBCA animations) also appear in the game, but aren't part of the
    2D pipeline. The 3D formats are well-documented externally; the
    NFTR font format is what we used to dig into the game's character
    rendering, but it's standard and not worth a separate page here.
  </p>
</FieldGuideLayout>
